 "drivingQuestion": "Is the mishna's rule that individual offerings render substitutes universal?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Rule limited to animal offerings",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "temurah.14a.5",
   "daf": "14a",
   "sentenceId": 4,
   "he": "גְּמָ׳ קׇרְבַּן יָחִיד עוֹשֶׂה תְּמוּרָה כּוּ׳. וּכְלָלָא הוּא? וַהֲרֵי עוֹפוֹת דְּקׇרְבַּן יָחִיד, וְאֵין עוֹשֶׂה תְּמוּרָה! כִּי קָתָנֵי — בִּבְהֵמָה קָתָנֵי.",
   "en": "«GEMARA:» The mishna teaches that «offerings of an individual render» a non-sacred animal exchanged for the offering «a substitute.» The Gemara asks: «And» is «this» an established «principle?» Does every offering of an individual render a non-sacred animal exchanged for it a substitute? «But» what about «birds,» i.e., a dove or a pigeon, «which» are brought as «an offering of an individual, but they do not render» a non-sacred animal exchanged for them «a substitute?» The Gemara answers: «When» the mishna «teaches» that offerings of an individual render a non-sacred animal exchanged for the offering a substitute, it «is teaching» this only «with regard to an animal» offering, not a bird offering."
  }
 ],
